\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The snake equation is the algebraic engine powering every pregroup type reduction. The compact closure axiom (\autoref {eq:eq-4-3}) rendered by DisCoPy: \textbf  {left panel} shows the zigzag \((\mitvarepsilon _n \otimes 1_n) \circ (1_n \otimes \miteta _n)\) where a Cup \(\mitvarepsilon {}\) composed with a Cap \(\miteta {}\) forms a snake; \textbf  {right panel} shows the identity wire \(1_n\) it equals. Verified computationally via \texttt {diagram.normal\_form()\ ==\ Id(Ty(\textquotesingle {}x\textquotesingle {}))}. Each pregroup contraction (noun canceling with verb argument) is an instance of \(\mitvarepsilon {}\); the snake equation guarantees that cup-cap pair insertions and removals leave derivations invariant (cf.~\autoref {sec:categorial-grammar}).}}{29}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:discopy-snake}{{13}{29}{The snake equation is the algebraic engine powering every pregroup type reduction. The compact closure axiom (\autoref {eq:eq-4-3}) rendered by DisCoPy: \textbf {left panel} shows the zigzag \((\varepsilon _n \otimes 1_n) \circ (1_n \otimes \eta _n)\) where a Cup \(\varepsilon {}\) composed with a Cap \(\eta {}\) forms a snake; \textbf {right panel} shows the identity wire \(1_n\) it equals. Verified computationally via \protect \texttt {diagram.normal\_form()\ ==\ Id(Ty(\textquotesingle {}x\textquotesingle {}))}. Each pregroup contraction (noun canceling with verb argument) is an instance of \(\varepsilon {}\); the snake equation guarantees that cup-cap pair insertions and removals leave derivations invariant (cf.~\autoref {sec:categorial-grammar})}{figure.13}{}}
