\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Prompt injection is detectable as a categorical type violation in the case interaction graph. In the legitimate diagram (top), authority flows NOM→INS→ACC per \autoref {eq:eq-9-1}. Prompt injection (bottom) inserts a cross-category morphism \(\mitphi {}\) that attempts to promote Data (ACC) to commanding Agent (NOM) while demoting the System Prompt (LOC) to passive data. The resulting diagram fails to commute, and the adversary's illicit type-reassignment is flagged as a categorical exception by the case-theoretic firewall---transforming prompt injection from an open-ended jailbreak game into a decidable type-checking problem. Generated programmatically from \texttt {src/visualization/security\_plots.plot\_type\_violations()}.}}{68}{figure.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:security-violations}{{25}{68}{Prompt injection is detectable as a categorical type violation in the case interaction graph. In the legitimate diagram (top), authority flows NOM→INS→ACC per \autoref {eq:eq-9-1}. Prompt injection (bottom) inserts a cross-category morphism \(\phi {}\) that attempts to promote Data (ACC) to commanding Agent (NOM) while demoting the System Prompt (LOC) to passive data. The resulting diagram fails to commute, and the adversary's illicit type-reassignment is flagged as a categorical exception by the case-theoretic firewall---transforming prompt injection from an open-ended jailbreak game into a decidable type-checking problem. Generated programmatically from \protect \texttt {src/visualization/security\_plots.plot\_type\_violations()}}{figure.25}{}}
